WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.127 --> 00:00:04.994
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.994 --> 00:00:08.930
My generation is the junk food generation.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:17.050
Raised on Gushers and Capri Sun, double
stuffed Oreos, and dollar menu McNuggets.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:21.470
Forget fun size,
we ate fruit by the foot, by the foot.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:27.364
High fructose corn syrup was added
to Coke in 1980 when the first

00:00:27.364 --> 00:00:28.905
millenials were born.

00:00:28.905 --> 00:00:33.046
And, in what cannot be a coincidence
Dunkaroos were discontinued the year I

00:00:33.046 --> 00:00:35.240
graduated from college.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:40.990
Suddenly I was an adult,
food science has give us the signals that

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:46.650
humans evolved to crave, salt, sugar, fat.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:51.470
But without the substance they were once
associated with in nature, real nutrition.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:57.031
Junk food's engineered so
that you literally can't resist,

00:00:57.031 --> 00:01:00.444
once you pop you can't stop
isn't empty marketing.

00:01:00.444 --> 00:01:04.940
It's evolutionary science,
junk food is engineered.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:11.044
Junk food has divorced the signals
we crave from the substance

00:01:11.044 --> 00:01:15.041
our bodies need,
it has made us sick as a result.

00:01:17.493 --> 00:01:24.837
But social media, over the last decade and
a half, has given us something worse.

00:01:24.837 --> 00:01:29.575
Junk food for the soul,
millennials first we fell for

00:01:29.575 --> 00:01:32.640
Fun Dip then we fell for Facebook.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:40.320
Humans crave connection, deep,
personal connection with those around us.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.596
Dozens of scientific studies indicate
what we all intuitively know.

00:01:44.596 --> 00:01:48.665
When our lives are filled with
meaningful social interaction,

00:01:48.665 --> 00:01:52.145
we're happier, we're healthier,
we live longer.

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:57.810
So hypothetically, we should be lucky
to live in this age of connectivity.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:03.380
Where through text, and WhatsApp and
Instagram, I can feel connected to nearly

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:09.490
everyone I have ever met, and yet
we have this crisis of loneliness.

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:17.818
In the 70s and 80s between 10 and
20% of Americans said they regularly or

00:02:17.818 --> 00:02:22.468
frequently felt lonely,
by 2010 it was 40%.

00:02:22.468 --> 00:02:27.387
Between 1985 and
2004 the percent of Americans who said

00:02:27.387 --> 00:02:32.392
they did not feel like they had
a single friend they could rely on to

00:02:32.392 --> 00:02:37.700
discuss important matters more
than doubled from 20% to 43%.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:42.713
Not a single friend,
the fact that we are so connected, yet

00:02:42.713 --> 00:02:49.130
feel so alone, is only a paradox to
someone who's never used social media,

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:54.448
who's never spent a Saturday
night lying on their unmade bed,

00:02:54.448 --> 00:02:57.178
scrolling through Instagram.

00:02:57.178 --> 00:03:01.526
Because, obviously there's
a difference between the virtual

00:03:01.526 --> 00:03:04.027
time we spend together and the real.

00:03:04.027 --> 00:03:07.773
And after spending real
time with my friends,

00:03:07.773 --> 00:03:11.338
I don't feel empty, I don't feel guilty.

00:03:11.338 --> 00:03:15.088
I don't tell myself I'm going to stop
seeing my friends so much and if I do,

00:03:15.088 --> 00:03:18.913
it'll only be for five minutes and
only if I've been really productive.

00:03:18.913 --> 00:03:21.016
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:21.016 --> 00:03:23.627
&gt;&gt; Facebook, Instagram, Snapchat,

00:03:23.627 --> 00:03:28.935
they're engineered to give us the signals
we associate with friendship,

00:03:28.935 --> 00:03:33.500
sharing stories, liking pictures,
remembering a birthday.

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.790
But without the substance
of true connection,

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:43.303
we talk about Instagram celebrities as if
they're unique in creating these filtered,

00:03:43.303 --> 00:03:47.192
artificial versions of themselves online,
but we all do it.

00:03:47.192 --> 00:03:51.483
Celebrities just get paid for it,
we complain about fake news on Facebook,

00:03:51.483 --> 00:03:55.516
Facebook was built on fake news,
it was just usually about ourselves.

00:03:58.732 --> 00:04:03.727
It's no surprise that the heaviest
users of social media report twice

00:04:03.727 --> 00:04:07.888
as much social isolation as
those who don't use it at all.

00:04:07.888 --> 00:04:11.140
It's no coincidence that we're
the generation that invented FOMO.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:16.410
Of course I'm going to fear missing
out if I spend hours a day staring at

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:21.346
definitive evidence of me missing out.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:21.346 --> 00:04:22.183
&gt;&gt; At root,

00:04:22.183 --> 00:04:27.110
these technologies are designed for
our engagement but

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:31.524
not with each other, but
the technology itself.

00:04:31.524 --> 00:04:34.981
Sean Parker,
the first president of Facebook and

00:04:34.981 --> 00:04:40.840
the founder of Napster framed the question
as how to we, we being Facebook.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:45.354
Consume as much of your time and
conscious attention as possible?

00:04:45.354 --> 00:04:51.349
And his answer,
create a social validation feedback loop.

00:04:51.349 --> 00:04:55.433
When I post a picture, I keep
checking to see how many likes I get,

00:04:55.433 --> 00:05:00.600
every refresh is like another pull of
the social validation slot machine.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.581
And every like gives me that
little hit of dopamine,

00:05:03.581 --> 00:05:07.463
that little signal of friendship and
social approval that I crave.

00:05:09.203 --> 00:05:12.979
Eventually, it wears off, and
so I post another picture,

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:14.950
another quarter in the slots.

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:22.220
It's an addictive feedback loop that,
in Parker's words,

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:29.116
exploits vulnerability in human
psychology and keeps us on the platform.

00:05:29.116 --> 00:05:33.420
Once you pop you can't stop,
it makes me wonder,

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:38.137
if video games Candy Crush or
Call of Duty are similarly

00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:42.957
doing more to deliver the signals
of accomplishment and

00:05:42.957 --> 00:05:47.080
the substance of having
an impact in the world.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:54.602
It makes me wonder if dating apps
are doing more to deliver the signals

00:05:54.602 --> 00:06:01.353
of romantic interactions than the
substance of forming real relationships.

00:06:01.353 --> 00:06:07.298
It even makes me wonder if music
festivals and $34 workout classes,

00:06:07.298 --> 00:06:11.063
are now giving us
the signals of community and

00:06:11.063 --> 00:06:15.733
purpose that we once looked for
in spiritual practice.

00:06:15.733 --> 00:06:19.432
I mean, it's called soul cycle,

00:06:19.432 --> 00:06:25.695
all the signals we crave, but
do they have the substance?

00:06:25.695 --> 00:06:30.482
A friend as defined by Facebook
is no more a real friend than

00:06:30.482 --> 00:06:35.283
Doritos Tacos Locos is real food,
it's empty calories.

00:06:35.283 --> 00:06:39.320
It leaves us hungrier for
true connection than when we started.

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:44.740
Mark Zuckerberg, for his part,
is not blind to this phenomenon.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:50.692
I remember this year he wrote, he now
wants us to have more meaningful social

00:06:50.692 --> 00:06:55.096
interactions, so the time we spend
on Facebook is time well spent.

00:06:55.096 --> 00:06:57.533
As a result of the changes he's made,

00:06:57.533 --> 00:07:03.350
total time on the platform actually
fell by 15 minutes per person per week.

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:07.502
Is it a healthier Facebook, or
just a slightly less addicting one?

00:07:09.327 --> 00:07:13.015
Yes technology can create community, but

00:07:13.015 --> 00:07:18.655
only if it's facilitating interaction and
not crowding it out.

00:07:18.655 --> 00:07:24.461
If junk food capsizes the problem,
maybe food can also offer the solution.

00:07:24.461 --> 00:07:29.946
Because if we're the junk food generation,
we're also the farmer's market generation.

00:07:29.946 --> 00:07:33.790
We recognized that junk food was
delivering an empty signal and

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:35.990
was making us sick.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:40.197
And it's that sort of radical
reorientation that we've taken with

00:07:40.197 --> 00:07:44.423
regards to food that we now need to
extend to our social life as well.

00:07:44.423 --> 00:07:47.914
In the end,
we have to be willing to be dissatisfied.

00:07:49.618 --> 00:07:52.850
Because the greatest threat is not
that we get nothing from social media.

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:57.584
But that we get just enough signal
never to hunt for that substance.

00:07:59.525 --> 00:08:00.672
That Farmville,

00:08:00.672 --> 00:08:05.190
makes us feel just productive enough
that we never produce anything.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:10.530
That Tinder makes dating just easy enough
that we never form a relationship.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:17.260
That Instagram makes us feel just
connected enough to leave us truly alone.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:23.923
A few weeks ago, one of my best friends
from growing up had a birthday.

00:08:23.923 --> 00:08:28.775
And we're all scattered across the country
now but over our WhatsApp group we sent

00:08:28.775 --> 00:08:32.643
out our happy birthdays, and
I typed one out under the desk during

00:08:32.643 --> 00:08:37.230
class with a long string of emojis and
it was funny, it was fine, it was nice.

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:43.030
But that night back in my room,
sitting on the edge of my bed,

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:46.640
I picked up my phone and I called him.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.841
And we conferenced in our other friends,
and we talked and we laughed, and

00:08:52.841 --> 00:08:58.482
we made fun of each other, we consoled
each other, and it was great, it was real.

00:08:58.482 --> 00:09:02.602
At the end of the call,
after my other friends had hopped off,

00:09:02.602 --> 00:09:06.341
he said to me,
Mike it's just great to hear your voice.

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:11.782
I still eat gushers sometimes,

00:09:11.782 --> 00:09:17.350
I still go on Facebook more
often than I'd like to admit,

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:22.918
but now at least I can treat it for
what it is, junk food for

00:09:22.918 --> 00:09:28.460
the soul.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:32.585
[MUSIC]

